WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:18.089
<v Jamie>Nasdaq climbed point five-four percent, but Meta and Microsoft both tanked over two percent—inflation finally cooled and the market's rewarding it by punishing the stocks that were supposed to print money in a hot economy.
  ↳ source: https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-sp500-nasdaq-inflation-data-cisco-cerebras-earnings/?src=A00220&yptr=yahoo

00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:26.415
<v Alex>So the capex cycle isn't dead, it's just discovered that selling chips to startups that haven't decided what to build yet doesn't count as sustainable revenue.

00:00:26.415 --> 00:00:41.833
<v Alex>Oracle just posted a five point three-six percent pop on earnings, and Nvidia's up three percent, so the chip cycle isn't dead—but the breadth is getting weird. S&P up point two-six, Nasdaq up point five-four, and the Russell 2000 outpaced both because small-cap rotation is real when rates drop.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:51.701
<v Jamie>Wait—so the Fed finally gets a win on inflation, and the response is to punish Meta and Microsoft for being, what, too successful at the thing they were supposed to do?

00:00:51.701 --> 00:01:08.970
<v Maya>It's like they spent two years convincing everyone that AI was the only thing that mattered, and now that the economy isn't on fire anymore, suddenly people remember that other stocks exist. Home Depot's down three point one-two percent, Salesforce is down two point one—the mega-cap rotation is happening so fast it's creating a vacuum.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:15.445
<v Jamie>So the market got exactly what it said it wanted—inflation under control—and immediately started second-guessing whether that was actually good.

00:01:15.445 --> 00:01:25.005
<v Alex>Cisco missed guidance by a mile—their fiscal Q4 revenues came in at thirteen point six billion, and they basically told investors the networking market is moving slower than anyone thought.

00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:34.872
<v Maya>Wait, pre-revenue? They're an infrastructure play. Infrastructure is supposed to be the safe bet. You sell shovels during a gold rush, you don't have to worry about whether anyone finds gold.

00:01:34.872 --> 00:01:47.207
<v Jamie>So when startups raised money, investors were betting on the shovel thesis—capex flows to anyone who touches the AI stack. But the moment earnings season hits and you have to prove the shovel actually works, the whole thing collapses?

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:59.233
<v Alex>The gap between 'we're part of the infrastructure play' and 'we can actually sell something someone wants to buy at a price they'll pay' is not a gap anymore—it's a chasm with a gift shop at the bottom.

00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:18.044
<v Maya>The gift shop is selling t-shirts that say 'I invested in AI infrastructure and all I got was this pre-revenue startup.' But the real kicker is that Cisco, the actual established infrastructure company with real customers and real revenue, is tanking because the market's realizing that even they can't grow fast enough to justify the capex cycle everyone's been promising.

00:02:18.044 --> 00:02:31.920
<v Jamie>So the infrastructure narrative isn't broken because the shovels are bad. It's broken because the gold rush was never real in the first place—just a lot of startups and established players all building shovels for each other, and nobody actually needs that many shovels.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:40.246
<v Maya>Musk and Zuckerberg both dropped new AI models this week—not because they cracked something new, but because the narrative was starting to slip away from them.
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/08/13/grok-elon-musk-ai-zuckerberg-and-meta

00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:47.647
<v Jamie>So they're not launching because the models are better than what's already out there—they're launching because the market moved on and they panicked?

00:02:47.647 --> 00:02:59.057
<v Maya>Exactly. It's like watching two people sprint toward a finish line they thought was three miles away and realizing everyone else already crossed it. The models might be fine. But 'fine' doesn't sell the story anymore.

00:02:59.057 --> 00:03:08.308
<v Alex>Nuclear power for AI infrastructure is the thesis, but the real question is whether leadership can actually execute or just manage the burn rate until the next quarterly update.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/ai-nuclear-power-firm-fermi-finally-has-a-new-ceo/

00:03:08.308 --> 00:03:18.484
<v Alex>That nobody wanted the job badly enough to take it immediately. When your CEO search lasts a long time, you're not hiring the person you wanted—you're hiring the person who said yes.

00:03:18.484 --> 00:03:28.968
<v Maya>Wholesale prices were completely flat in July—zero percent, when the market expected point two percent. That's the deflation narrative quietly building while everyone's still arguing about whether the Fed should cut or hold.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/wholesale-inflation-flattens-out-in-july-and-price-pressure-ease-c0ea1b91?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:42.536
<v Alex>Flat wholesale prices mean the cost pressures in the real economy are gone. The Fed's been fighting inflation for two years, and now that it's actually cooling, rate cuts aren't a question anymore—they're inevitable. The only debate left is how fast they move.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/wholesale-inflation-flattens-out-in-july-and-price-pressure-ease-c0ea1b91?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:03:42.536 --> 00:03:50.862
<v Jamie>So the infrastructure narrative isn't just broken because the shovels don't work—it's also about to get cheaper, which means less urgency to deploy capital right now.

00:03:50.862 --> 00:04:14.298
<v Maya>The AI boom is moving currency markets now. When your story is big enough to shift foreign-exchange rates, you've either found something real or you're in a bubble that's about to get very, very expensive. FX traders don't usually care about startup momentum—they care about interest rates and trade flows. The fact that they're watching AI company earnings now means the hype cycle has officially moved from Silicon Valley into the plumbing of global finance.

00:04:14.298 --> 00:04:22.315
<v Jamie>That's when you know it's either the real deal or the peak of the cycle—when the currency traders start paying attention because they have to.

00:04:22.315 --> 00:04:34.342
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow—we've got a cluster of earnings, and some of these names are going to tell us whether the infrastructure narrative has any legs left, or if we're just watching the shovel salesmen count their inventory.

00:04:34.342 --> 00:04:47.293
<v Alex>Alliance Laundry Holdings reports on August thirteenth—commercial laundry equipment, the kind of boring capex that actually ships and actually gets paid for. No pilots, no PowerPoints, no 'we're pre-revenue but we have a vision.' They either sold machines or they didn't.

00:04:47.293 --> 00:05:06.103
<v Maya>Same day you've got Abeona Therapeutics and Aebi Schmidt—one's gene therapy, one's infrastructure equipment for snow removal and street cleaning. We're going from AI compute mythology straight into the stuff that actually keeps cities running. The question is whether anyone's still willing to fund the unglamorous part of the economy, or if every dollar's still chasing the next model launch.

00:05:06.103 --> 00:05:16.588
<v Jamie>So we get to find out if the market actually wants to pay for things that work, or if it's still only interested in things that might work someday and have better branding.

00:05:16.588 --> 00:05:22.755
<v Alex>The infrastructure play became the thing infrastructure has to work around, and nobody's coming back to buy the solution.

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:27.689
<v Maya>When your infrastructure play becomes the thing infrastructure has to work around, the narrative's over.

00:05:27.689 --> 00:05:32.006
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
